{{also|Axe|axé}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Accetta.jpg|thumb|An axe]]
From {{inh|en|enm|ax}}, {{m|enm|axe}}, {{m|enm|ex}}, from {{inh|en|ang|æx}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*akusi}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*akwisī}}, probably from a {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂egʷsih₂|t=axe}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-|t=sharp, pointed}}. Compare {{cog|de|Axt}}, {{cog|nl|aks}}, {{cog|da|økse}}, {{cog|is|öxi}}, and also {{cog|la|ascia}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|ax||largely|_|US}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ăks}}, {{IPA|en|/æks/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-axe.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æks|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[tool]] for [[fell]]ing trees or [[chop]]ping wood etc. consisting of a heavy <!-- [[metallic]] (material is not essential, the axes used to be of stone in the stone age --> [[head]] [[flatten]]ed to a [[blade]] on one side, and a <!-- usually wooden (material is not essential) --> [[handle]] attached to it.
# An [[ancient]] [[weapon]] consisting of a <!-- [[metallic]] --> [[head]] that has one or two [[blade]]s and a long [[handle]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[dismissal]] or [[rejection]].
#: {{syn|en|chop|pink slip|sack|boot}}
#: {{ux|en|His girlfriend/boss/schoolmaster gave him the '''axe'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[drastic]] [[reduction]] or [[cutback]].
#: {{co|en|the {{w|Beeching cuts|Beeching '''Axe'''}}}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|author=w:Bob Dylan|title=w:Tangled Up in Blue|album=Blood on the Tracks|passage=I had a job in the great North Woods / Workin' as a cook for a spell / But I never did like it all that much / And one day the '''axe''' just fell}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=1994
|author=Tony Scotland
|title=The Empty Throne: The Quest for an Imperial Heir in the People's Republic of China
|url=https://archive.org/details/emptythronequest00scot/
|publisher=w:Penguin Books
|ISBN=0-14-017869-4
|OCLC=221410579
|OL=7349476M
|page=103
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/emptythronequest00scot/page/103/
|text=But P'u-yi was nothing if not soft when it came to family, and he arranged for the young man to live with his uncle Beitzu P'u-hsiu in P'u-yi's old house in T'ien-ching. So Yü-t'ai was well clear of Ch'ang-ch'un when the '''axe''' fell in 1945.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 March 8|author=Howard Johnston|title=Was Marples the real railway wrecker?|journal=RAIL|issue=978|page=50|text=Back in 1963, how could Beeching advocate closure of the electrified Liverpool-Southport commuter route, just because its books didn't balance? The busy North London line between Richmond and Broad Street was also for the '''axe''', as was Leeds to Bradford and Ilkley.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|music}} A [[gig|gigging]] musician's particular instrument, especially a [[guitar]] in [[rock]] music or a [[saxophone]] in jazz.
# {{lb|en|finance}} A position, interest, or reason in buying and selling [[stock]], often with ulterior motives.
#: {{ux|en|A financial dealer has an '''axe''' in a stock that his buyers don't know about, giving him an advantage in making the most profit.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In the United States, some spell the weapon ''axe'' and the tool ''ax'' to distinguish them, though most people use the same spelling for both senses.

